## GitHub overlay image build proposal

- Updated OpenSpec package `add-github-overlay-image-build` for building the
  fork image from GitHub and pulling it on the server. Docker construction and
  backend compilation run only on a canonical `main` push or manual dispatch
  selected on exact main. The user decided that no general, unit, static, or
  workflow-check tests run automatically on feature pushes, PRs, main, or
  schedules; tests are run manually for a specific task. The main image
  workflow verifies the actual container artifact before publication and has
  no unit-test gate; the manifest-merger precompile test is not part of the
  required build. It uses the existing official
  UI/backend overlay and pinned ARM64 base digest
  `sha256:754b5e50361dc2802f0b6576456e72a5163cdc991378ce3f212a2f90b771eb96`,
  verifies one local candidate before GHCR authentication, and publishes the
  full-SHA and `:main` tags with recorded manifest digest and source/base/run
  metadata. The operator pulls the main image, compares its digest, starts
  with `docker compose up -d --no-build`, and retains prior-digest rollback.
- The smoke contract targets the direct Next UI listener (`20128`):
  with `REQUIRE_API_KEY=true`, unauthenticated `GET /api/v1/models` returns
  HTTP 401 with `error.code: "AUTH_002"` from client-API auth middleware,
  before the catalog route handler.
- Proposal, design, tasks, capability delta, and repo TODO track the same
  main-only image trigger and no-automatic-tests policy. PR #11, including the
  reviewed runtime changes and eligible completed OpenSpec archives, merged
  at `3a484460`. GHCR's first publication
  defaults private; after a successful main publish, the owner switches the
  package to public and anonymous pull is verified. The workflow leaves
  visibility unchanged. Automated production deployment is separate follow-up
  scope.
- Final manual verification passed 13/13 focused tests, scoped ESLint,
  Prettier, actionlint (0 findings), the workflow audit (209 findings against
  a baseline of 233), strict OpenSpec validation (7/7), and
  `git diff --check`. Independent low-effort review of the final workflow
  policy found no correctness issues. Code Simplifier reviewed the trigger/job
  changes and removal of unit-test gates; no additional simplification was
  needed. These tests are task-specific manual checks, not automatic CI gates.
- Keep `api-route-typecheck.yml`,
  `test-quarantine.yml`, and `release-acceptance.yml` manual-only; keep other
  routine check workflows disabled and preserve only the fork image workflow
  as active. The server playbook now documents how to preserve this after
  upstream sync.
- GitHub settings update completed: before PR #11 merged, the Actions API
  listed 27 workflows, with 25 upstream workflows already disabled and API
  Route Typecheck plus the fork image workflow active. API Route Typecheck was
  disabled; after merge the API reports 29 workflows total, 28 disabled, and
  only the fork image workflow active. Feature push run `37121740387` and PR run
  `37121831813` from the old automatic checks were cancelled. API typecheck PR
  run `37121831856` had completed before its workflow was disabled. The merged
  source makes the three auxiliary workflows manual-only.
- First main image workflow run `37124029642` failed after 4m17 in
  `npm run build:backend`; V8 emitted repeated ineffective mark-compacts and
  allocation failures at an effective old-space limit near 1043 MiB. No image
  was published. The Dockerfile had no explicit `NODE_OPTIONS`, and the build
  helper preserves inherited options instead of applying its 8192-MiB
  fallback. The failed run did not measure process RSS, cgroup peak, or Node
  version. A prior successful remote compile of the same backend used a
  12-GiB heap with about 15.72 GB measured peak; the public ARM runner states
  16 GB.
- PR #12 (`a06fcfb3`) applied the builder-stage-only
  `NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=12288` before `npm run build:backend`;
  it does not alter the runtime stage or shared build helper.
- Second main run `37125217705`, after PR #12, failed after 6m18 while
  prerendering `/_global-error`. Next reported
  `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'use')`, followed by
  `Export encountered an error on /_global-error/page`; the Next worker exited
  1 at about 214.4 seconds. The prior V8 heap-limit error did not recur. The
  workflow did not log in to GHCR, publish an image, or change production.
  Read-only diagnosis found that the Dockerfile set `NODE_ENV=development` for
  `npm ci` and left it set during backend compilation; the installed Next CLI
  preserved it, and build logs showed React development warnings. The exact
  minified callsite was not isolated. The builder now sets
  `NODE_ENV=production` after `npm ci` and before `npm run build:backend`; the
  final runtime stage starts from the fresh pinned base. Stage-order inspection,
  `git diff --check`, and Code Simplifier review passed; no tests were rerun and
  no local image build was performed. PR #13 (`b6cfc9ad`) carried this
  correction to main. Run `37126849223` completed the native ARM64 candidate
  build and backend compilation in about 9 minutes (13:38:48–13:47:41 UTC),
  clearing the prior compile failures in this run. Candidate verification then
  failed with `EACCES: permission denied` unlinking
  `/tmp/omni-overlay-data-uR7ulC/cache/openrouter-provider-stats.json`. No
  earlier error appeared in available output; read-only diagnosis tied EACCES
  to a host bind mount at `/app/data`, where container `node` UID 1000 created
  the file and runner UID 1001 could not unlink it. Because `finally` cleanup
  may have masked a primary verification failure, the log does not prove
  preceding checks passed. Selected correction: use container-scoped
  `/app/data` tmpfs mode `1777`, preserve default image `USER`, and report
  primary verification and all cleanup errors without hiding either. A
  cleanup-only error still blocks publication. The verifier and focused manual
  regression tests are complete: all three added regressions failed before the
